StrataFS is a forkable, lazy loaded, POSIX compliant file system that can be used on top of any ordered transactional KV store + a blob store.

StrataFS is designed for an agent first world.
Creating worktrees and even cloning on a fresh instance become O(1).
This works because content is lazy loaded based on when it is read,
allowing your codebase to stream in as your agent + its tools read it, without any special configuration.

StrataFS is strongly consistent and durable by default.
On macOS, FSKit (macOS 27.2) forces three gaps in strong consistency, due to underlying bugs:

- A write that fails (another mount writing the same file at the same moment, or a store failure) can be read back on
  the mount it failed on, by reads waiting on it, until its data is dropped a moment later.
- Lookups directly in the volume root can miss names another mount creates there, since FSKit cannot drop the root's
  cached names (FB25243414).
- On one mount, a chmod, link or rename of a file fails with EBUSY while a write to that file is in flight there.

On Linux, FUSE (kernel 6.12) forces these gaps in strong consistency, due to underlying limits:

- A read can see the data of a write in flight on its own mount before the write is stored, and after a write that
  fails, a shared mapping there keeps showing its data until the page is read again.
- A read that runs while another mount changes the file's size can stop at the size the file had before, or return
  zeros past a truncate, since Linux asks for a file's size and its data in two separate requests.
- On one mount, lookups keep finding a name its own unlink or rename drops until that change returns, since only a
  notify that takes the directory's lock, which the change holds, can drop the name.
- While a write that grows a file is in flight on a mount, the kernel there takes in no attributes from any reply, so
  its reads can use a size from before another mount's change until the write returns.
- On one mount, a stat can return a file's size from before another mount's change that already returned, while another
  stat of the file there takes the new size in, as the kernel marks the attributes current before it stores the size.
- On one mount, a stat can return a file's attributes from before another mount's change, when the kernel let go of
  the file and looks it up again as the change lands, since FUSE can hand over the forget after the new lookup. Kernels
  before 6.12.110 and 6.13 can also take a lookup's reply in after letting go of the file during the lookup.

StrataFS itself has these open gaps, on macOS and Linux alike:

- When two mounts change the same file at the same moment, one of the changes fails with EBUSY. On Linux, a change can
  also fail with EBUSY while its mount drops a copy for any other mount's change, since the mount cannot see which of
  the kernel's locks are held.
- On one mount, a rename, unlink or open can rarely fail with ESTALE while another mount changes the same file, since
  the kernel would take lookups from different moments as one; it tries once more first.
- A file open on one mount is deleted as soon as another mount unlinks it, so later reads, writes and fstat on the open
  descriptor fail with ESTALE: the mount that unlinks it deletes it once its own kernel lets go of it, and nothing
  records the opens other mounts hold.

StrataFS is completely written in Rust for maximum performance and portability. Strata can be used on Linux, macOS, and even WebAssembly.

This project is currently in active development, and should not be relied on (yet).
